<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 1px solid #a7d9ed;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Rules of the Road</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate a discussion on a group project. Concept: Interpret graphical information for classmates.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a group discussion on transportation options by encouraging participation and managing turn-taking, using supportive gestures and prompts to guide the flow of ideas (e.g., gestures toward the speaker to indicate their turn and asks, "Who would like to share their thoughts on the best route?").
Can interpret a transportation map by pointing out key locations and using simple explanations (e.g., points to the bus stop in Boquete and explain that it leads to the David terminal, where travelers can catch a bus to Panama City or other destinations).
Can explain public transportation rules using simple words and two languages when necessary (e.g., describes "fare" in both English and familiar language).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays an image of a busy street with various forms of transportation (bus, car, pedestrian crossing). Students are asked: "What transportation do you see? What rules do you think apply here?" The teacher elicits answers using vocabulary like 'traffic light', 'sidewalk', 'passenger', 'vehicle', 'route'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a simple rule from the image, using modal verbs. For example, pointing to a pedestrian crossing: "You <b>have to</b> stop at red lights." Pointing to a bus: "You <b>should</b> consider taking the bus; it's often <b>cheaper</b> and more <b>convenient</b>." The teacher demonstrates clarifying terms, like "fare" (e.g., "The 'fare' is the money you pay to ride the bus, also known as 'pasaje' in Spanish."). The teacher also focuses on the stress in 'transportation' and 'convenience'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you *have to* stop at a red light, or is it optional?" (Expected answer: "You have to.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is taking the bus *always* the fastest, or can it just be more affordable?" (Expected answer: "It can be more affordable.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple infographic or a short illustrated guide about "Benefits and Rules of Public Transportation." This could include icons for 'safety', 'convenience', 'cost-efficiency' and rules like 'wear seatbelt', 'tap in', 'get off at the right stop'. The visual includes a simple map showing a bus route from Boquete to David. It explicitly uses vocabulary like 'benefits', 'safety', 'convenience', 'fare', 'schedule', 'route', 'get on', 'get off'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will interpret graphical information and short textual explanations related to public transport, preparing them to facilitate discussion on a group project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students answer gist questions based on the infographic in pairs.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are two main benefits of public transportation shown in the infographic?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one rule passengers must follow when using the bus?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where can travelers catch a bus to Panama City, according to the map?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using modal verbs for advice and obligation ("You should take the bus." "You have to stop at red lights."), comparatives/superlatives ("The train is faster than the bus." "The plane is the fastest."), and phrasal verbs related to transportation ("get on," "get off," "hop on"). Pronunciation practice for stress in 'passenger', 'schedule', 'highway' and distinguishing /æ/ in 'tap' vs /eɪ/ in 'tape'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Rule Match-Up.</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of different transportation situations (e.g., someone waiting for a bus, a car at a stop light, someone wearing a seatbelt). They match these pictures to sentences using target grammar. Example: "You _____ your seatbelt for safety." (must/have to wear). "It's _____ to ride a bike with a helmet." (safest). "The bus route is _____ than driving." (more efficient).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Explaining the Map.</b> In pairs, students are given a simplified transportation map (e.g., a bus route from David to Panama City with key stops and connections). Partner A explains a section of the route or a rule from the infographic to Partner B, using modals for advice/obligation and at least two transportation vocabulary words. Partner B then rephrases Partner A's explanation or asks for clarification. They must use phrases like "Who would like to share their thoughts on the best route?" and encourage participation. The teacher reminds students to focus on word stress (e.g., "passenger", "schedule") and vowel sounds (/æ/ in 'tap' vs /eɪ' in 'tape').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation Focus:</b> The teacher models sentences like "The <b>passenger</b> <b>taps</b> in when boarding the bus and <b>taps</b> the badge to their luggage." and "I <b>hope</b> you can <b>hop</b> on the bus before it leaves." Students repeat chorally and individually, focusing on the highlighted sounds and stress patterns.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: Public Transport PSA Project Discussion.</b> Students work in their established groups for the Public Transport Video PSA. Their task is to facilitate a group discussion, mediating ideas and information about their PSA project. Each group is given a specific aspect of their PSA to discuss and explain to their group members (e.g., the main message, target audience, a specific rule they will highlight, a benefit to feature). They must interpret their preliminary script/storyboard or a given graphic (e.g., a simple route map, a comparison chart of transport options) and explain it clearly to their peers. Students must use mediation strategies such as encouraging participation ("What are your thoughts on this rule?"), managing turn-taking (gesturing to a speaker), and explaining concepts using simple words, possibly clarifying in two languages if needed (e.g., "The 'fare' (pasaje) is the cost to ride."). They emphasize safety and suggest convenience, using modals ("You <b>should</b> explain why wearing a seatbelt is <b>safest</b>.") and comparatives ("The bus is <b>more efficient</b> for this route.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining, facilitating discussion, interpreting visual/textual information, using appropriate pragmatic functions and sociolinguistic protocols.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Group Presentation Snippets and Self-Reflection.</b> Each group briefly presents a summary of their PSA discussion, with one student acting as the facilitator, explaining a key point from their project. For instance, a student might explain the "safest" way to "get on" and "get off" a bus, or interpret a simple graphic from their PSA storyboard. The teacher observes and uses a checklist for formative assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitates group discussion by encouraging participation and managing turn-taking (e.g., using prompts and gestures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interprets graphical information (e.g., map, storyboard) clearly and simply for classmates.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., 'safety', 'convenience', 'route', 'fare') and grammatical structures (modals for advice/obligation, comparatives) correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces multisyllabic words with correct stress and distinguishes between target vowel sounds (e.g., /æ/ vs /ɑː/, /ɒ/ vs /əʊ/).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explains concepts using simple words and clarifies in two languages when necessary (e.g., 'fare').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I helped my group by explaining..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next time, I want to improve my ability to facilitate a discussion by..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about explaining information or facilitating the discussion to your group?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using English to talk about transportation rules and benefits help your group understand the project better?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Students effectively used modals and comparatives when explaining rules and benefits. The visual aids (infographic, map, PSA storyboard) were very helpful for initiating mediation tasks."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Some students struggled with managing turn-taking naturally. I need to provide more explicit sentence starters or role-play scenarios for facilitating discussion and encouraging participation in future mediation tasks. More focused practice on phrasal verbs in context could also be beneficial."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Transportation: Rules of the Road" theme. The next unit will build upon the 21st Century Skills Project (PSA) by focusing on digital literacy and presentation skills, allowing students to finalize and present their Public Transport Video PSAs, integrating the linguistic competencies acquired throughout this theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>